AirPlay plugin for Kalinka
==========================

This package installs the Python wheel under /opt/kalinka/wheels, and
shairport-sync 5.5.2 (with AirPlay 2) and nqptp 1.2.8, built for this
architecture on Debian 13, under /usr/libexec/kalinka-plugin-airplay. Their complete
source is in /usr/share/kalinka-plugin-airplay/source. The
kalinka-server-restart dpkg trigger asks the server to restart; Kalinka's
bootstrap then installs the wheel into its managed environment. The plugin
is disabled by default.

The package adds a kalinka.service drop-in granting CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE:
nqptp, which the plugin runs for AirPlay 2, binds UDP ports 319 and 320.
Every plugin in the server's process can then bind ports below 1024.

Before enabling AirPlay:

* Keep the distribution's own shairport-sync and nqptp services disabled:
  `systemctl disable --now shairport-sync nqptp`. A running shairport-sync
  would play to the sound card itself, and a running nqptp holds the ports
  AirPlay 2 needs, so Kalinka would offer classic AirPlay only.
* Allow AirPlay through a firewall, as shairport-sync's TROUBLESHOOTING.md
  lists: UDP 5353 (mDNS), TCP 7000 (the plugin's service port), UDP 319-320
  and 6000-6009, and the ephemeral range 32768-60999 over TCP and UDP.
* Select a renderer through Kalinka, enable AirPlay, and pick the speaker in
  the AirPlay menu of an iPhone, iPad or Mac.

Removal uninstalls the plugin from /opt/kalinka/venv using Kalinka's standard
prerm hook, and removes the drop-in. The plugin supervises shairport-sync and
nqptp itself and restarts them after an unexpected exit; it installs no
service of its own.

Build and operation instructions:
https://github.com/Kalinka-Player/kalinka-plugin-airplay
